WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.040
Hello everyone. Nice to see you.

00:00:02.040 --> 00:00:06.120
Really exciting to be here
helping to kick off day two of DevCon.

00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:10.040
And I'm impressed
by the number of people in the room today.

00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:14.520
You have made it through the weather,
the tube strikes and you're here.

00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:17.520
I know I had my own battle to get here
this morning as well.

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:23.520
I'm Marc Sloan, I'm a member of the
product team here at Tessl, and I'm here

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:28.760
today at a developer focused conference
to talk about non developers.

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:34.120
Now, I don't know if there are any other
non developers in the room here today.

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:36.480
I'm thinking yeah there we go.

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:39.720
Product managers, designers executives
you know

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:42.880
the people who traditionally wouldn't
touch code.

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:47.720
Although I think we're all we're all
turning into developers these days right.

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:50.160
This talk

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:54.960
is about the work that they create,
but also it is for everyone, rest assured,

00:00:55.040 --> 00:00:59.160
because as we talk increasingly
about the context

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:02.160
that we give agents, coding agents,

00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:04.960
a lot of that,
the origination of that context

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:08.600
comes from non developers,
comes from product and design teams.

00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:13.280
And so what I'm going to talk about
today is the creation of that context,

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:19.080
the challenges we face in exposing that
to our coding agents and their harnesses.

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:23.080
And what solutions to those challenges
might look like.

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:28.440
Now, over the last 24 hours,
the conversation at Dev Con has been

00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:32.960
pretty much about one thing, and that's
giving agents the right context.

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:36.680
I think it's been super interesting
working in this area

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:41.400
over the last 6 to 12 months,
and we've seen the methodology

00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:44.200
we use to do that, evolving
from spec driven

00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:47.640
development, where we right up front
all the context that the agents

00:01:47.720 --> 00:01:49.760
are going to need in order
to get the task done

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:53.560
to context engineering,
which is kind of where we are just now,

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:56.880
and how that's evolving
into harness engineering.

00:01:57.480 --> 00:02:02.800
And the tools are maturing really fast
in this area, but a lot of

00:02:02.840 --> 00:02:08.040
it has been focused on context that
originates and lives within the code base.

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:12.040
And the natural form
that that context has been taking

00:02:12.040 --> 00:02:14.240
recently has been in the form of skills.

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:17.640
Now, I don't think I need to convince
anybody here of the

00:02:18.280 --> 00:02:21.040
the value of skills
and how powerful they are.

00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:24.440
There was a great quote
in one of the talks yesterday

00:02:24.440 --> 00:02:27.640
about how the simplest solution is often
the right one,

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:31.960
and the fact that the markdown file
is so flexible and adaptable

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:36.160
makes it the natural place
to be able to put code based context.

00:02:36.280 --> 00:02:41.000
I think another powerful aspect of
it is the fact that that context

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:45.080
can live alongside the code
that it's describing, that it lives

00:02:45.080 --> 00:02:50.120
within the repo, that it's managed,
it's versioned and can be subject

00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:54.320
to evaluations and tests and all that
good stuff that comes with the harness.

00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:59.520
And we're still figuring out
how to manage that kind of context.

00:02:59.560 --> 00:03:00.320
Right?

00:03:00.320 --> 00:03:03.040
All the stuff we've been talking about
with harness engineering,

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:06.360
the features that you see in the Tessl
product, are all about figuring out

00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:09.880
how we turn skills and context into code.

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:13.840
So to kick us off

00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:15.640
and to kick off this conversation
about product

00:03:15.640 --> 00:03:19.560
and design context, I'm
going to walk through a typical example,

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:22.200
the kind of thing I'm sure everybody in
this room is familiar to.

00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:23.720
Everybody in this room.

00:03:23.720 --> 00:03:28.040
We're going to describe a scenario here
where we've got a company that has a web

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:32.520
app product, and a customer has asked
the product manager

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:37.640
to put an export button
on the dashboard in their product.

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:42.480
Now, the export feature was something
that was hidden behind the settings menu,

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:45.840
and there was a couple of layers deep,
so it was very difficult to find.

00:03:45.880 --> 00:03:49.840
Customer wasn't aware
that it existed, and requested

00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:52.840
this to be in a more prominent position.

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:55.920
On the surface,
this seems like the perfect

00:03:55.920 --> 00:04:01.160
kind of job to give to an Agent
a Linear ticket that describes it.

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:04.640
Put a figma design in there
that shows where the button should go

00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:05.640
and what it should look like.

00:04:05.640 --> 00:04:07.160
All that good stuff

00:04:07.160 --> 00:04:11.560
fired off to your agents, have it produce
a PR job done, customers happy.

00:04:11.680 --> 00:04:12.640
The feature got delivered.

00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:14.640
The customer tried it out and said,
it's great.

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:16.680
So cool.

00:04:16.680 --> 00:04:18.440
This is the world we live in right now.

00:04:18.440 --> 00:04:21.080
We can just send these things off
to agents.

00:04:21.080 --> 00:04:24.080
And of course, the agent wasn't building
this blind.

00:04:24.320 --> 00:04:28.240
It had access to a bunch of skills
that had been developed in advance

00:04:28.280 --> 00:04:32.440
to instruct the agent on how it should be,
thinking about the architecture

00:04:32.440 --> 00:04:35.920
of the code,
about the front end components that live

00:04:35.920 --> 00:04:39.120
within that, the testing
and CI requirements,

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:42.560
and how to make use of the API
to get the data.

00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:47.640
And of course, this is a cutting edge
state of the art company.

00:04:47.640 --> 00:04:50.560
So they're using Tessl
to manage all of this stuff.

00:04:53.120 --> 00:04:54.840
So problem solved.

00:04:54.840 --> 00:04:57.680
Why do we need to be thinking
about product and design context?

00:04:57.680 --> 00:05:01.360
Well in this scenario
the agent got one thing wrong.

00:05:01.640 --> 00:05:06.400
It actually ended up using a generic react
button component

00:05:06.400 --> 00:05:07.840
that lived within the code base,

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:11.040
and not the more specific export
button components.

00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:13.800
That was the right choice here,

00:05:13.800 --> 00:05:16.800
and this might seem like a trivial error,

00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:20.760
but encoded within that export
button component

00:05:20.920 --> 00:05:25.520
are the accessibility requirements
that this organization adheres to.

00:05:25.600 --> 00:05:29.480
Interaction patterns
that make sense for this kind of async,

00:05:29.840 --> 00:05:32.080
the async operation
of this kind of button.

00:05:32.080 --> 00:05:35.960
And this was all missed by the agent
in creating this.

00:05:36.320 --> 00:05:39.720
And what's challenging here is that as
far as the harness was concerned,

00:05:39.840 --> 00:05:41.040
everything went smoothly.

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:44.920
The agent got a ticket handed off to it,
did the job, created a PR

00:05:44.960 --> 00:05:48.280
that passed,
and the feature went out to the customer.

00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:50.720
The valuations
that were set up on the skills

00:05:50.720 --> 00:05:54.480
and the other aspects of the harness
all seem to be working well,

00:05:54.680 --> 00:05:57.600
but the product and design context
was missing

00:05:57.600 --> 00:06:00.600
from the agent
in this particular situation.

00:06:01.120 --> 00:06:04.800
And this can have real world consequences
because while the customer was happy

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:08.920
out of the bat in this situation,
it might be that down the line.

00:06:09.080 --> 00:06:11.960
The app then ends up
failing an accessibility audit

00:06:11.960 --> 00:06:14.920
that delays a critical
deal and that kind of stuff.

00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:17.880
So we might

00:06:17.880 --> 00:06:22.000
ask ourselves, well, how should
the agents have learned about that?

00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:24.640
How can it find the right
context to do it?

00:06:24.640 --> 00:06:29.400
Well, the context was in the design system
in Figma this whole time.

00:06:30.520 --> 00:06:34.720
It turns out that the design system team
had thought this stuff through

00:06:34.720 --> 00:06:38.520
and already thought about how this button
should be created, how

00:06:38.520 --> 00:06:41.480
it should be designed, and
how the interaction should work with it.

00:06:41.480 --> 00:06:47.200
In fact, the Figma design that was in that
original linear ticket did correctly use

00:06:47.200 --> 00:06:51.320
the export button design component,
but the agent just didn't pick this up.

00:06:51.400 --> 00:06:54.800
It used the Figma design
as a visual treatment, and then from that,

00:06:54.840 --> 00:06:58.640
inferred that it should be using
a generic button that it customized.

00:07:01.560 --> 00:07:06.440
So as we think about building
agent harnesses,

00:07:06.440 --> 00:07:10.440
as we've been doing
for the last 24 hours in this conference,

00:07:10.800 --> 00:07:14.560
we might ask ourselves, where does product
and design context fit into this?

00:07:14.720 --> 00:07:19.640
Now, the way I think about Agent Harnesses
is that it's a layer that wraps

00:07:19.640 --> 00:07:24.600
the code base itself, and that layer
contains skills, evaluations,

00:07:24.600 --> 00:07:29.040
hooks, policies, all this other stuff
that we're working on at the moment.

00:07:29.040 --> 00:07:32.680
And we're using tools like Tessl to manage

00:07:34.560 --> 00:07:36.040
the product and design context.

00:07:36.040 --> 00:07:39.960
I see existing in this layer surrounding
the harness, at least right now.

00:07:40.680 --> 00:07:43.080
And it's not just
that it's a layer outside of the harness,

00:07:43.080 --> 00:07:45.800
but this data doesn't live
in the code base. By design.

00:07:45.800 --> 00:07:49.680
It lives in the third party
tools that product managers and designers

00:07:49.680 --> 00:07:52.680
and so on are using day to day,

00:07:53.240 --> 00:07:53.880
every day.

00:07:53.880 --> 00:07:58.040
Context is getting created in these third
party tools, and the code base

00:07:58.040 --> 00:08:01.160
is blind to it, and therefore the agent
and the agent harness as well.

00:08:02.240 --> 00:08:05.560
And it makes sense that it lives
in those tools, because that's the tools

00:08:05.560 --> 00:08:09.040
where product managers and designers are
more are spending their time.

00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:11.240
Designers
are going to continue to work in Figma

00:08:11.240 --> 00:08:15.040
because it has a canvas and
a great set of tools for creating designs.

00:08:15.080 --> 00:08:19.000
PMS will keep working in linear
and notion, and more customer data

00:08:19.000 --> 00:08:21.160
is going to continue to live in CRM,

00:08:22.800 --> 00:08:24.120
and for as long as

00:08:24.120 --> 00:08:27.880
that keeps happening, we're going to have
important contexts that agents

00:08:27.920 --> 00:08:30.960
need in order to build the right things
and do the right job

00:08:31.000 --> 00:08:34.080
living outside the code base,
and not just the context.

00:08:34.400 --> 00:08:37.160
Agents need to get the job done,
but also the context.

00:08:37.160 --> 00:08:40.760
We need to understand
whether the job got done correctly.

00:08:41.440 --> 00:08:45.000
So challenge one is the fact that product
and design context

00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:48.000
is important to agents,
but it lives outside the code base.

00:08:49.200 --> 00:08:51.760
If we go back to our example,
let's imagine

00:08:51.760 --> 00:08:54.840
the team has picked up on this
and decided to try and

00:08:54.840 --> 00:08:58.240
bring that context into the code base
in order to solve this problem.

00:08:58.600 --> 00:09:00.000
They've set up storybook.

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:03.000
They've gone through the design system
in Figma and made sure

00:09:03.000 --> 00:09:06.760
all of the design components that are
there are well represented in storybook,

00:09:06.760 --> 00:09:10.560
with the appropriate documentation
and interaction patterns and so on.

00:09:10.920 --> 00:09:13.920
This means the next time
a request like this comes in,

00:09:14.160 --> 00:09:18.320
the agent picks up the right component
correctly and gets the job done.

00:09:18.520 --> 00:09:19.600
Perfect.

00:09:19.600 --> 00:09:24.040
This is all working,
but the world keeps evolving

00:09:24.040 --> 00:09:26.840
and the product and design
context isn't static.

00:09:26.840 --> 00:09:30.800
You can imagine in this situation
something like this where a new deal comes

00:09:30.800 --> 00:09:35.560
in, and with this new deal comes
a new set of accessibility requirements.

00:09:35.560 --> 00:09:37.920
Let's say
let's say it's a government agency.

00:09:39.080 --> 00:09:40.360
The PM picks this up and

00:09:40.360 --> 00:09:43.760
produces a notion document
that outlines what those requirements are.

00:09:44.320 --> 00:09:47.760
The the design team update Figma
and the design components

00:09:47.760 --> 00:09:49.800
there to make sure
that they are fully compliant.

00:09:49.800 --> 00:09:52.800
And while all that's going
on, the code base is falling

00:09:52.800 --> 00:09:56.440
increasingly out of sync with what's
going on in product and design land,

00:09:56.840 --> 00:10:01.160
and the agent is still producing features
using the outdated design system.

00:10:02.160 --> 00:10:06.320
And so for as long as we accept
that product and design context

00:10:06.360 --> 00:10:09.520
is going to live outside the code base,
then we're going to have

00:10:09.520 --> 00:10:13.360
this problem of the two
falling out of sync with each other.

00:10:13.520 --> 00:10:16.960
And I've talked about this
in one direction so far,

00:10:17.320 --> 00:10:22.040
with the product and design context
evolving as the code stays the same.

00:10:22.040 --> 00:10:26.360
But the opposite is also true,
especially as we have more and more agents

00:10:26.480 --> 00:10:28.040
being thrown into the code base

00:10:28.040 --> 00:10:32.280
and building new components from scratch
and updating existing components.

00:10:32.280 --> 00:10:36.800
We're also going to find ourselves
in the situation where the app itself

00:10:36.800 --> 00:10:39.560
has a whole bunch of components
the design team know nothing about,

00:10:39.560 --> 00:10:41.520
and so we need to be able
to move the context

00:10:41.520 --> 00:10:43.320
in the other direction for them as well.

00:10:46.920 --> 00:10:47.600
Now, in all of

00:10:47.600 --> 00:10:50.600
this, you might be thinking,
isn't this a solved problem?

00:10:50.600 --> 00:10:53.600
Should all of these tools have MCP servers
today?

00:10:53.600 --> 00:10:58.360
Isn't it just a case of connecting the
agent to the MCP server and we're done?

00:10:59.360 --> 00:11:02.360
That's true to an extent, but

00:11:02.520 --> 00:11:06.000
the fact that we need to have this
live connection to third party product

00:11:06.000 --> 00:11:10.120
and design context also introduces
a set of new challenges, especially as we

00:11:10.160 --> 00:11:12.400
now start to think about the agent 
harness

00:11:12.400 --> 00:11:13.360
as being something

00:11:13.360 --> 00:11:17.400
that's managed and controlled, and
we want to be improving all of the time.

00:11:17.880 --> 00:11:21.880
We now have a live connection that we need
to maintain and work around the rate

00:11:21.880 --> 00:11:24.880
limits of that imposes cost constraints

00:11:24.920 --> 00:11:27.960
and the third party availability.

00:11:28.640 --> 00:11:31.880
We also get into a situation
where the evals

00:11:31.880 --> 00:11:33.400
and things like that,
that we're setting up on.

00:11:33.400 --> 00:11:35.440
The skills
and the context in the code base

00:11:35.440 --> 00:11:39.200
is now reliant on third party context
that's constantly changing.

00:11:39.280 --> 00:11:42.320
That doesn't necessarily
have a canonical source of truth.

00:11:42.320 --> 00:11:45.120
And of course, that's the problem
with product and design context.

00:11:45.120 --> 00:11:46.760
It is constantly up to date.

00:11:46.760 --> 00:11:50.440
That's never really a definition of done
there.

00:11:50.440 --> 00:11:53.920
It can be in an incomplete state
by virtue of its nature.

00:11:54.400 --> 00:11:57.400
And so if we're building harnesses
that are reliant on that,

00:11:57.400 --> 00:12:00.200
we have to be very careful
about how we make that work.

00:12:02.520 --> 00:12:03.280
So let's

00:12:03.280 --> 00:12:06.280
pause for a moment to reflect
on what we've established here.

00:12:06.880 --> 00:12:10.680
I'm saying that for agents
to work effectively and for the harnesses

00:12:10.680 --> 00:12:14.760
that use them to work effectively,
they need to take into account product

00:12:14.760 --> 00:12:15.920
and design context.

00:12:15.920 --> 00:12:19.680
And this, by its nature, lives
outside of the code base.

00:12:20.160 --> 00:12:25.320
It, by its nature, falls
out of sync with the code base over time,

00:12:25.320 --> 00:12:29.360
and that trying to maintain
a live connection between the agent

00:12:29.360 --> 00:12:32.800
and that context introduces
a whole set of new challenges

00:12:32.800 --> 00:12:35.800
that we have to deal with.

00:12:36.000 --> 00:12:37.880
Now, everything I'm talking about

00:12:37.880 --> 00:12:40.960
might seem theoretical,

00:12:41.000 --> 00:12:45.680
or, you know, we're still just trying
to get a head around context in the code

00:12:45.720 --> 00:12:48.680
base around skills
and how we manage skills and so on.

00:12:48.680 --> 00:12:51.560
Is this really such a big deal
at the moment?

00:12:51.560 --> 00:12:52.880
Well, everything I've described

00:12:52.880 --> 00:12:56.640
so far are real challenges
that I've faced in my life.

00:12:56.640 --> 00:13:01.240
Prior to Tessl, when I was working
at Figma on their dev mode product.

00:13:03.200 --> 00:13:04.280
For those who don't know, dev

00:13:04.280 --> 00:13:09.240
mode is a feature in Figma,
which allows designers to hand designs off

00:13:09.240 --> 00:13:12.440
to developers
human developers in this case, back in

00:13:12.760 --> 00:13:15.240
back in the Stone age,
these were the tools we were using

00:13:15.240 --> 00:13:20.920
and it gives the developer
lots of great context about the designs

00:13:20.920 --> 00:13:25.440
that they can use in manually creating
the code to implement those designs.

00:13:25.680 --> 00:13:29.360
And over the last couple of years,
we've seen that feature

00:13:29.360 --> 00:13:32.880
evolve into Figma, MCP server,
the live Connection.

00:13:32.920 --> 00:13:33.720
Right.

00:13:33.720 --> 00:13:38.600
And what we're doing here is just taking
that exact same design context,

00:13:38.600 --> 00:13:41.680
but giving it directly to an agent via
the MCP server.

00:13:42.360 --> 00:13:45.680
And it was in building this
that we started

00:13:45.680 --> 00:13:48.680
to see all of those problems
that I've been describing.

00:13:48.680 --> 00:13:51.760
We would see agents picking up the designs

00:13:51.760 --> 00:13:55.680
and doing a fantastic job
in creating front end code

00:13:55.680 --> 00:13:59.600
that looked just like the design
that functioned as the designer intended.

00:13:59.600 --> 00:14:03.960
But the developers would look at the code
and it would have made up

00:14:03.960 --> 00:14:06.400
a bunch of components
when they otherwise existed,

00:14:06.400 --> 00:14:09.160
or used them in the wrong way,
or completely missed that

00:14:09.160 --> 00:14:12.200
there was a design system
that it should have been working towards.

00:14:12.240 --> 00:14:15.120
These were real problems that
I encountered with customers every day.

00:14:16.160 --> 00:14:17.680
And so we started.

00:14:17.680 --> 00:14:21.200
We realized quite quickly
that we needed to find a way to bridge

00:14:21.200 --> 00:14:25.600
that product and design context gap
with what agents were doing.

00:14:25.640 --> 00:14:29.400
And to that end, an early feature
we started working on to address

00:14:29.400 --> 00:14:31.520
this was Figma Code Connect product.

00:14:31.520 --> 00:14:34.560
And here is a tool that explicitly

00:14:34.560 --> 00:14:39.240
allowed design system teams
to connect the design components

00:14:39.240 --> 00:14:42.880
in that design system
with their equivalent in the code base.

00:14:43.400 --> 00:14:48.360
Now I don't want to go into and code
connect too much.

00:14:48.360 --> 00:14:53.320
The point here is that these aren't
new problems.

00:14:53.320 --> 00:14:56.400
These were problems
over the last several years that product

00:14:56.440 --> 00:14:59.880
teams and design teams
and devs are dealing with day to day.

00:14:59.880 --> 00:15:01.360
Already.

00:15:01.360 --> 00:15:04.760
We were already dealing with a product
and design context to agent

00:15:04.760 --> 00:15:08.280
harness problem before we even defined
half of those terms,

00:15:08.680 --> 00:15:12.680
and struggling
to find the right solution for that.

00:15:13.240 --> 00:15:18.360
And it's from working on that over
the last couple of years.

00:15:18.360 --> 00:15:23.360
And now with my role at all, thinking
about how those kinds of things apply

00:15:23.360 --> 00:15:26.440
to harness engineering,
that I think there are some useful lessons

00:15:26.440 --> 00:15:29.760
we can take from that as we think about
solutions to that problem.

00:15:30.760 --> 00:15:33.200
The first lesson is that

00:15:33.200 --> 00:15:37.960
in building this Code Connect tool,
we were aware that there was that

00:15:37.960 --> 00:15:41.520
that connection between product
and design context and what's in the code

00:15:41.560 --> 00:15:44.920
base required
dedicated maintenance in enterprises.

00:15:44.920 --> 00:15:48.480
There are entire teams whose sole job
is to make sure that that connection

00:15:48.480 --> 00:15:51.800
is up to date and is makes sense,

00:15:51.800 --> 00:15:54.800
and that there is equivalence
between the two.

00:15:55.640 --> 00:15:59.720
And one of the reasons those teams exist,
and that maintenance is required,

00:15:59.720 --> 00:16:03.040
is because the design context
and the product context

00:16:03.080 --> 00:16:06.320
is constantly falling out of sync
with the code base.

00:16:06.360 --> 00:16:09.480
Both are evolving at their own pace
for their own reasons,

00:16:09.480 --> 00:16:13.360
and it requires dedicated effort
to keep the two reconciled.

00:16:13.400 --> 00:16:16.680
And one of the learnings
was that sometimes

00:16:16.680 --> 00:16:20.120
it's not desirable, actually,
to keep the two completely in sync.

00:16:20.160 --> 00:16:23.240
There's an acceptance that they will,
of course, have to be out of sync because

00:16:23.240 --> 00:16:27.080
of the pace of design work
and product work and dev work and so on.

00:16:27.080 --> 00:16:30.520
And it's the job of this team
just to make sure the status quo

00:16:30.560 --> 00:16:33.760
is functional and working
and to keep track of that over time.

00:16:34.360 --> 00:16:37.560
And I think that's broadly true
not just of design and code context,

00:16:37.560 --> 00:16:39.880
but product and design context in general.

00:16:41.160 --> 00:16:44.160
The third
lesson, I think, is interesting in that

00:16:44.280 --> 00:16:48.000
as someone working in product management
on this product,

00:16:48.040 --> 00:16:51.600
I was also very much aware of the
the business

00:16:51.600 --> 00:16:54.600
and the structural constraints
around the tools we were building.

00:16:55.800 --> 00:17:00.000
Figma as an organization was very
protective of its design system IP.

00:17:00.080 --> 00:17:01.600
It didn't just want to give away the keys

00:17:01.600 --> 00:17:03.920
to the house,
and so we had to be very careful

00:17:03.920 --> 00:17:07.480
about finding the right balance
between supporting agents

00:17:07.480 --> 00:17:11.120
and the developers using them,
but also not giving everything away

00:17:11.160 --> 00:17:14.000
and destroying the moat
that they've built over time.

00:17:14.000 --> 00:17:16.600
And I think that's true
across all of these product

00:17:16.600 --> 00:17:19.600
and design SaaS providers, not just Figma.

00:17:22.040 --> 00:17:26.600
So as I spend my days
currently thinking about harness

00:17:26.600 --> 00:17:29.120
engineering
and how we make sure that agents

00:17:29.120 --> 00:17:32.320
have all the information they need
to do a great job for developers,

00:17:33.880 --> 00:17:34.560
and I take

00:17:34.560 --> 00:17:37.680
on board these lessons that I've learned
from my experience in trying to solve this

00:17:37.760 --> 00:17:39.680
in other organizations.

00:17:39.680 --> 00:17:43.360
I see maybe three directions
that the harness

00:17:43.400 --> 00:17:45.480
can take in order to solve this.

00:17:45.480 --> 00:17:48.480
And if we've got a bit of time,
there's a sneaky fourth one as well.

00:17:49.480 --> 00:17:52.360
The first is that we can see these tools

00:17:52.360 --> 00:17:56.120
perhaps opening up,
and we're already seeing this.

00:17:56.120 --> 00:18:00.680
The number of these third party tools that
have MCP servers just now is exploding.

00:18:00.920 --> 00:18:05.240
And we're also seeing them expand
with their own APIs and cliffs and so on.

00:18:05.360 --> 00:18:08.440
I think you still do see restrictions
and rate limits in place.

00:18:08.440 --> 00:18:11.880
And we're also seeing this surface
being an opportunity

00:18:11.920 --> 00:18:15.520
for those organizations to create
new monetization pathways as well.

00:18:15.560 --> 00:18:19.560
So I don't think the solution here
is as straightforward as all

00:18:19.560 --> 00:18:21.760
the data will just become available
for everything.

00:18:21.760 --> 00:18:25.000
But I do think this is going to get us
some of the way there.

00:18:25.240 --> 00:18:28.520
And it's something where we're in
the middle of at the moment.

00:18:30.760 --> 00:18:32.520
Another option is the

00:18:32.520 --> 00:18:37.120
idea that there is an agent
that sits between the third party

00:18:37.120 --> 00:18:42.480
product and design context,
and the harness agent in particular.

00:18:42.760 --> 00:18:45.360
The lesson I learned
was about this connection,

00:18:45.360 --> 00:18:48.920
requiring dedicated maintenance 
and pruning over time.

00:18:48.920 --> 00:18:53.240
And increasingly we did see those teams
using agents to a system.

00:18:53.400 --> 00:18:57.320
So it's entirely reasonable
that they start to get to a place

00:18:57.320 --> 00:18:59.560
where they can automate
a lot of that work.

00:18:59.560 --> 00:19:03.280
And I think as we think as well
about the harness itself

00:19:03.320 --> 00:19:07.040
acquiring its own agent
in order to build and manage that harness,

00:19:07.040 --> 00:19:10.920
you know, Tessl's IO agent,
check it out.

00:19:12.360 --> 00:19:14.640
We can see the role that

00:19:14.640 --> 00:19:17.960
those kinds of agents
might have expanding to cover,

00:19:18.000 --> 00:19:19.400
not just managing the context

00:19:19.400 --> 00:19:23.280
within the code base, but also the context
that lives outside of it as well.

00:19:26.000 --> 00:19:28.240
And finally,

00:19:28.240 --> 00:19:30.160
another option here is that this context

00:19:30.160 --> 00:19:33.600
that lives outside the code base
and is unmanaged and versioned,

00:19:33.640 --> 00:19:38.360
perhaps just gets swallowed by the repo,
and we'll see it making inroads into it,

00:19:38.360 --> 00:19:41.800
and then being able to be managed
by the harness directly.

00:19:42.360 --> 00:19:46.280
And I think what's interesting with this
is that there are some challenges here.

00:19:46.280 --> 00:19:49.800
Certainly
we're going to have to still meat products

00:19:49.800 --> 00:19:52.960
and designers
and other non developers where they're at,

00:19:53.120 --> 00:19:56.760
because designers are still going
to want to design on a canvas regardless

00:19:56.760 --> 00:19:58.760
of where that data ends up living.

00:19:58.760 --> 00:20:01.640
Likewise with CRMs and things like that.

00:20:01.640 --> 00:20:03.600
So we need to meet them where they're at.

00:20:03.600 --> 00:20:07.800
But it could be
that there's just still so much value

00:20:07.800 --> 00:20:13.280
in having this context within the harness,
that we see an organic pull of that data

00:20:13.280 --> 00:20:14.480
into a place where it can be

00:20:14.480 --> 00:20:18.400
managed and versioned and evaluated
and all this great harness stuff.

00:20:19.680 --> 00:20:20.480
I also think what's

00:20:20.480 --> 00:20:25.560
interesting about this direction
is it also calls to the fact

00:20:25.560 --> 00:20:31.000
that we as non developers
are increasingly looking like developers.

00:20:31.120 --> 00:20:35.720
In fact, there are several talks yesterday
and today specifically about this topic

00:20:35.720 --> 00:20:38.840
and about product managers and designers
and so on, creating

00:20:38.880 --> 00:20:41.880
PRS and actively contributing
to the code base.

00:20:42.040 --> 00:20:46.800
This might be the impetus for us to fold
the kind of context

00:20:46.800 --> 00:20:49.800
that we're generating day by day
into the repo.

00:20:51.320 --> 00:20:53.320
And with that in mind,

00:20:53.320 --> 00:20:56.400
what I want to do is go back and revisit

00:20:56.400 --> 00:21:01.920
our example from earlier
and think about the situation where

00:21:01.960 --> 00:21:05.560
perhaps a developer wasn't involved
in this at all.

00:21:07.240 --> 00:21:11.400
We can imagine a PM like myself
had that conversation with a customer.

00:21:11.760 --> 00:21:16.400
We transcribed that conversation using
Granola, fed that into Claude in Cowork.

00:21:16.480 --> 00:21:21.240
I have my own set of skills there
that are able to look through transcripts

00:21:21.240 --> 00:21:26.240
like that and identify features from them,
turn those features into linear tickets.

00:21:26.520 --> 00:21:30.400
I have a skill that encodes my roadmap
and make sure that this fits my roadmap

00:21:30.400 --> 00:21:35.280
and has the right priority,
and one thing to call out here is that I,

00:21:35.320 --> 00:21:41.120
as a non developer, might be developing
these skills outside of the code base.

00:21:41.160 --> 00:21:42.320
These aren't versioned.

00:21:42.320 --> 00:21:44.800
These don't have
these aren't accessible to my harness.

00:21:44.800 --> 00:21:46.720
They're not aware of them.

00:21:46.720 --> 00:21:49.440
So we need to make sure that

00:21:49.440 --> 00:21:52.320
this might be the first way that we bring

00:21:52.320 --> 00:21:55.440
some of that context into the code base
to manage that kind of thing.

00:21:55.880 --> 00:22:00.080
And of course, here
Claude turns the feature

00:22:00.080 --> 00:22:02.640
from the granola transcript
into a linear ticket

00:22:02.640 --> 00:22:04.320
that then fires off
that flow from earlier.

00:22:04.320 --> 00:22:07.600
The feature gets built
and the customer is happy.

00:22:08.600 --> 00:22:10.160
Except

00:22:10.160 --> 00:22:13.200
what the PM didn't know
is that there's a reason

00:22:13.200 --> 00:22:16.440
this export feature was hidden behind
various settings.

00:22:16.440 --> 00:22:17.120
Menu.

00:22:17.120 --> 00:22:20.480
Because of some architectural decision
that was made sometime in the past,

00:22:20.480 --> 00:22:25.000
that made exporting the customer's data
a hugely expensive task.

00:22:25.040 --> 00:22:26.960
And therefore,
we didn't want this to be something

00:22:26.960 --> 00:22:30.680
that was widely available
and operating with high bandwidth.

00:22:31.120 --> 00:22:34.000
This causes a PR
with a huge number of changes, and

00:22:34.000 --> 00:22:40.240
all of this is invisible to the PM, who's
happily tapping away in their computer,

00:22:40.320 --> 00:22:41.520
clapping themselves in the back

00:22:41.520 --> 00:22:44.680
for the fact that they're able to push PRS
and make features by themselves.

00:22:44.840 --> 00:22:50.760
And what's missing here is
I know personally from my own experience

00:22:50.760 --> 00:22:55.440
managing technical teams,
when I'm deciding which features to work

00:22:55.440 --> 00:23:00.080
on, I massively value
the feedback and conversation

00:23:00.080 --> 00:23:03.080
that I'm having with my team
to let me know.

00:23:03.120 --> 00:23:03.800
Help me understand

00:23:03.800 --> 00:23:07.120
the technical effort that goes into
creating some of these features.

00:23:07.280 --> 00:23:09.560
That helps me understand
the technical debt

00:23:09.560 --> 00:23:12.560
that we might be creating
as a result of this.

00:23:12.560 --> 00:23:15.800
And it can be just as valuable
for a product manager to know

00:23:15.800 --> 00:23:19.400
when not to build something
than to just look at a backlog

00:23:19.400 --> 00:23:23.520
as its do list that we just need
to throw agents at and work through.

00:23:24.480 --> 00:23:27.960
And so
another challenge here is that so far,

00:23:27.960 --> 00:23:31.720
I've been talking about bringing product
and design context to coding agents.

00:23:31.720 --> 00:23:35.720
But we also need to think about this
the other way around, and bringing

00:23:36.160 --> 00:23:40.000
coding context to the kind of decision
making that's happening

00:23:40.000 --> 00:23:43.000
outside of the code base,
but that's still affects it.

00:23:43.240 --> 00:23:46.280
And so one way this might evolve is

00:23:46.520 --> 00:23:50.880
we keep talking about coding agents
and the harnesses that enable them,

00:23:50.880 --> 00:23:54.600
but perhaps it's going to be more like
each of the different roles within

00:23:54.600 --> 00:23:58.160
a product team will have their own agent
and their own harness.

00:23:58.160 --> 00:24:01.080
And these harnesses
are going to have to talk to each other.

00:24:01.080 --> 00:24:04.080
And maybe this is
what the solution ends up looking like.

00:24:05.800 --> 00:24:09.000
Now, it's not clear which direction
these things are going to go

00:24:09.000 --> 00:24:10.720
in, and we're obviously going to see
a lot of progress

00:24:10.720 --> 00:24:13.720
being made on this front in the next
6 to 12 months.

00:24:13.760 --> 00:24:17.120
I think the thing that's clear to me
is that product

00:24:17.120 --> 00:24:20.960
and design context
is critically important for coding agents.

00:24:20.960 --> 00:24:24.000
And as we work on harnesses
for coding agents, certainly

00:24:24.000 --> 00:24:27.000
over the next couple of months,
we need to take that into account

00:24:27.000 --> 00:24:28.440
and figure out the best way

00:24:28.440 --> 00:24:31.440
to overcome some of the challenges
that we've talked about today.

00:24:31.720 --> 00:24:34.520
I'm sure folks in this room
are actively working on this.

00:24:34.520 --> 00:24:37.920
I know I am as well, and I'm
really excited to be working in this area.

00:24:38.520 --> 00:24:41.280
And with that, I'd like to say
thank you so much.

00:24:41.280 --> 00:24:45.400
Being a great audience, I'm happy to
stick around and answer a few questions.

00:24:52.240 --> 00:24:52.800
Awesome.

00:24:52.800 --> 00:24:54.680
Thank you very much. Right.

00:24:54.680 --> 00:24:55.600
I'm going to try these.

00:24:55.600 --> 00:24:59.080
I'm going to try and run through these
tables and get get the mic to people.

00:24:59.080 --> 00:25:02.080
If anyone's got a question
please raise your hand.

00:25:04.160 --> 00:25:07.160
Any questions?

00:25:08.560 --> 00:25:09.280
Thanks.

00:25:09.280 --> 00:25:10.680
Thanks for the talk.

00:25:10.680 --> 00:25:14.240
My first question comes in if you feed

00:25:14.240 --> 00:25:18.120
in more context in addition
to what we're already feeding in.

00:25:18.160 --> 00:25:21.160
Like where do you draw the line
and how do you group them?

00:25:21.160 --> 00:25:25.040
When do you when do you feed
in a bit of context that's needed?

00:25:25.120 --> 00:25:28.480
I remember 
there was some board activities

00:25:28.480 --> 00:25:33.040
at my workplace where they were asking
what all should be added to the code base.

00:25:33.040 --> 00:25:36.800
So there was this whole wash
list of things, including design,

00:25:36.800 --> 00:25:39.360
and everybody
wanted to get everything in the code base

00:25:39.360 --> 00:25:43.160
because they want to feed all of that,
like, you know, slack conversations, teams

00:25:43.160 --> 00:25:46.320
conversations, your email, conversations
with your client.

00:25:46.360 --> 00:25:48.840
It's like, where do you draw?
Where would you draw that line?

00:25:48.840 --> 00:25:51.000
Because obviously you need context.

00:25:51.000 --> 00:25:52.960
But then context is king.

00:25:52.960 --> 00:25:56.640
But then which part of it is it's it's
quite subjective as well right.

00:25:56.920 --> 00:25:57.280
Yeah.

00:25:57.280 --> 00:25:59.800
So some,
some some of your thoughts on that.

00:25:59.800 --> 00:26:00.440
Yeah.

00:26:00.440 --> 00:26:04.600
It's you know, where do you draw the line
in terms of adding context to the agent

00:26:04.600 --> 00:26:07.160
when I'm advocating
not just for adding code

00:26:07.160 --> 00:26:09.200
based context,
but also product and design context,

00:26:09.200 --> 00:26:13.160
which is likely even larger
than what's in the code base.

00:26:13.320 --> 00:26:16.560
I mean, I think this is the core problem
of context engineering and harness

00:26:16.560 --> 00:26:19.440
engineering in general,
even if we just remove the product

00:26:19.440 --> 00:26:23.680
and design context question for now, it's
already a huge challenge to figure out

00:26:23.720 --> 00:26:27.560
how to encode all of the contexts
that might live within a repo

00:26:27.560 --> 00:26:31.880
and its architecture and its norms,
and so on, into something succinct enough

00:26:31.880 --> 00:26:35.640
that's relevant enough for the agent 
to do the right job.

00:26:35.680 --> 00:26:40.040
And part of the reason we are setting up
things like evaluation architectures

00:26:40.040 --> 00:26:45.200
and so on, is so that we can actively work
to get that context down into

00:26:45.200 --> 00:26:47.600
just the right size,
but also make sure that that context

00:26:47.600 --> 00:26:50.760
is actually doing a good job to evaluate
and so on.

00:26:51.200 --> 00:26:52.640
We're going
to have the exact same problem,

00:26:52.640 --> 00:26:54.440
I think, with product and design context.

00:26:54.440 --> 00:26:58.640
In fact, that makes it harder because it's
I think, as I've said today,

00:26:58.760 --> 00:27:02.200
by virtue,
it's messier, there's more of it.

00:27:02.280 --> 00:27:03.960
It's in lots of different places.

00:27:03.960 --> 00:27:05.240
And so

00:27:05.240 --> 00:27:08.520
I think we just need to well, for now,
we need to apply the same kind of tools.

00:27:08.520 --> 00:27:12.240
We need to figure out
which design context is signal

00:27:12.240 --> 00:27:15.960
versus noise, and perhaps have agents
to help us decipher that.

00:27:16.000 --> 00:27:19.760
We need to shrink that down into formats
that are enough to either

00:27:19.760 --> 00:27:23.560
live as skills in the code base,
or maybe there's some other methodology

00:27:23.560 --> 00:27:27.280
that we shrink this to
and have that be accessible to the agent.

00:27:27.320 --> 00:27:31.080
And then we need a set of evals
to make sure that our agent

00:27:31.080 --> 00:27:34.760
is actually being boosted by that context
and isn't being overwhelmed.

00:27:34.800 --> 00:27:38.160
So the short answer is,
I think we need to apply

00:27:38.160 --> 00:27:42.720
all of the same principles with code base
context to product and design context

00:27:42.760 --> 00:27:45.720
with that extra layer of difficulty
that comes with that, because

00:27:45.720 --> 00:27:47.160
of it being external

00:27:49.040 --> 00:27:50.160
trial and error and evaluate.

00:27:50.160 --> 00:27:53.120
Exactly.

00:27:53.120 --> 00:27:55.000
Probably got time for one more question
if there's

00:27:55.000 --> 00:27:58.000
any other questions in the room.

00:28:00.880 --> 00:28:02.320
If not, thank you very much.

00:28:02.320 --> 00:28:05.320
Please give it up for Mark again.

00:28:08.240 --> 00:28:08.440
Okay.

00:28:08.440 --> 00:28:10.360
We've got just under ten
minutes till the next session

